# Code conventions

## Context

`app/src` is TypeScript-strict (`noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is on) with a
consistent set of conventions across every file — see
`materials/architecture-brief.md`, "Conventions".

## Rule

- **Named exports only** — no `export default` anywhere in `app/src`.
- No `any` and no `@ts-ignore`/`@ts-expect-error`. If a type is genuinely
  unknown, model it properly (union, generic, `unknown` + narrowing).
- All state updates are **immutable**: build new objects/arrays with spread,
  `map`, `filter`, etc. Never mutate an existing array/object in place.
- Relative imports between `app/src` files keep the `.js` extension (matches
  every existing import, e.g. `"./types.js"`, `"./lib/text.js"`), even though
  the file on disk is `.ts`.
- Type-only imports use `import type { ... }` (or an inline `type` modifier,
  as in `import { initialState, type AppState } from "./types.js"`) —
  don't import a type as if it were a value.
- `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is on: indexing an array (`tasks[0]`) or looking
  up an optional record key yields `T | undefined`. Guard/narrow before use
  (as `reducer.test.ts` does with `toggled.tasks[0]?.done`) instead of
  asserting it away with `!`.
- File names are **kebab-case** (e.g. `text-utils.ts`, not `textUtils.ts`);
  type/interface names are PascalCase (matches existing `Task`, `AppState`).

## How to verify

1. `grep -rn "export default" app/src` returns no matches.
2. `grep -rnE "\bany\b|@ts-ignore|@ts-expect-error" app/src --include=*.ts
   --include=*.tsx | grep -vE ':[0-9]+:\s*//'` — the second `grep` strips out
   comment-only lines automatically (a matched line whose content, after the
   `file:line:` prefix, starts with `//`), so the check is a deterministic
   pass/fail instead of eyeballing it; returns no matches. Test files
   (`*.test.ts`/`*.spec.ts` and their `.tsx` counterparts) are included, not
   exempt from this rule.
3. `grep -rnoE "(from|import)\s+['\"]\.\.?/[^'\"]*['\"]" app/src
   --include=*.ts --include=*.tsx | grep -vE "\.js['\"]$"` (every relative
   import via `from`/`import`, either quote style, `./` or `../`, filtered
   down to those NOT ending in `.js`) returns no matches.
4. `cd app && npm run typecheck` passes (strict mode catches unintended `any`
   leaks, unguarded indexed access, and bad import resolution).
5. `find app/src -type f \( -name "*.ts" -o -name "*.tsx" \) -exec basename {} \;
   | grep -vE '^[a-z0-9-]+(\.(test|spec))?\.(ts|tsx)$'` returns no matches —
   every filename under `app/src` follows the kebab-case pattern
   (`^[a-z0-9-]+(\.(test|spec))?\.(ts|tsx)$`); any printed line is a violation.